        "ctx": "IELTS Academic Reading Passage 1 (Questions 1–13)\nYou should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 1–13, which are based on Reading Passage 1 below.\n\nURBAN MICROCLIMATES AND NATURE-BASED INFRASTRUCTURE\n\nAcross the globe, metropolitan regions are confronting the escalating phenomenon known as the Urban Heat Island (UHI) effect. Densely clustered concrete edifices, extensive asphalt roadways, and dark roof surfaces absorb solar radiation during daylight hours and re-radiate this thermal energy nocturnally. In major conurbations such as Tokyo, London, and New York, nocturnal temperatures in inner-city centers can be up to 7°C to 10°C higher than in adjacent rural hinterlands. This localized climatic alteration not only accelerates peak electricity demand for air-conditioning systems but also exacerbates respiratory ailments and cardiovascular stress among vulnerable urban demographics.\n\nTo counteract this thermal escalation, municipal urban planners and landscape architects are increasingly turning toward nature-based infrastructure solutions. Chief among these strategies is the deployment of green roofs and living vertical facades. Extensive green roofs, characterized by shallow substrate depths of 7 to 15 centimeters planted with drought-resistant sedum species, function as biological insulating blankets. Solar radiation that would otherwise heat concrete roof slabs is instead dissipated through evapotranspiration—the biological mechanism whereby plants draw water from the soil and release it into the atmosphere as water vapor.\n\nA landmark four-year empirical study conducted by the Singapore Building and Construction Authority revealed that vegetative green roofs reduce surface roof temperatures by up to 30°C and lower internal ambient ceiling temperatures by approximately 3.5°C during tropical midday peaks. Furthermore, vegetated roofs absorb between 60 and 80 percent of stormwater runoff during intense torrential downpours, significantly mitigating flash flood risks by buffering municipal storm sewer drainage capacity.\n\nA second critical component of urban microclimate mitigation involves strategic street canopy afforestation. Urban forestry research indicates that the geometry of street canyons—the ratio of building height to street width (H/W ratio)—fundamentally dictates wind circulation and solar shading. Deciduous shade trees planted along east-west street axes intercept direct beam solar irradiance before it strikes asphalt pavement, whereas in winter, leaf drop permits desirable solar heat gain. However, aerodynamicists caution that excessively dense, continuous tree canopies in narrow street canyons with high H/W ratios can impede the turbulent dispersion of vehicular exhaust, inadvertently trapping particulate pollutants at pedestrian breathing height.\n\nConsequently, contemporary bioclimatic urban design emphasizes balanced canopy porosity. By integrating stepped building setbacks, decentralized bioswales, and porous vegetative wind corridors, modern cities can optimize passive evaporative cooling while ensuring the continuous aerodynamic purging of ambient atmospheric pollutants.",
