        "ctx": "IELTS Academic Reading Passage 2 (Questions 14–26)\nYou should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 14–26, which are based on Reading Passage 2 below.\n\nBIOMIMICRY: ENGINEERING SOLUTIONS INSPIRED BY NATURE\n\nParagraph A\nFor over 3.8 billion years, biological evolution has functioned as nature's rigorous research and development laboratory. Species that populate the Earth today represent evolutionary triumphs that have resolved complex thermodynamic, structural, aerodynamic, and chemical challenges using minimal energy and zero toxic synthetic residues. The discipline of biomimicry—a term popularized by natural sciences author Janine Benyus—formalizes the conscious emulation of nature's genius to solve human engineering and industrial design dilemmas.\n\nParagraph B\nPerhaps the most celebrated structural example of biomimetic architecture is the Eastgate Centre, a mid-rise shopping and office complex in Harare, Zimbabwe, designed by architect Mick Pearce. Conventional commercial glass towers in sub-Saharan Africa consume colossal quantities of electrical power for mechanical air conditioning. Pearce turned instead to the self-cooling mounds built by indigenous Macrotermes bellicosus termites. These termites cultivate subterranean fungus gardens that must be maintained at an exact temperature of 30.5°C despite external atmospheric fluctuations ranging from freezing at night to over 40°C at midday. The termites achieve this homeostatic stability through a complex matrix of passively convective flues and vents. By replicating this chimney system with concrete ducts and floor slabs, the Eastgate Centre maintains comfortable thermal equilibrium using 90 percent less energy than comparable neighboring edifices.\n\nParagraph C\nIn fluid dynamics and transportation engineering, marine biology has provided equally revolutionary breakthroughs. Humpback whales (Megaptera novaeangliae), despite weighing up to 40 metric tons, execute remarkably agile underwater acrobatic turns while pursuing schooling fish. Biomechanical analysis revealed that this maneuverability is enabled by bumpy, irregular tubercles along the leading edge of their pectoral flippers. Aerodynamic wind-tunnel tests conducted at the United States Naval Academy established that these tubercles disrupt fluid laminar flow, generating vortices that delay aerodynamic stall and reduce drag by 32 percent while increasing lift by 8 percent. Engineers have since adapted this 'tubercle effect' to redesign wind turbine blades, commercial airplane wings, and industrial cooling fans, dramatically boosting electrical output in low-wind conditions.\n\nParagraph D\nIn materials science, researchers have long marveled at the superhydrophobic self-cleaning properties of the sacred lotus leaf (Nelumbo nucifera). Water droplets that fall upon a lotus leaf do not flatten or adhere; instead, they bead into near-perfect spheres and roll off effortlessly, picking up dust and fungal spores along the way. Scanning electron microscopy demonstrated that this 'Lotus Effect' is not caused by chemical waxiness, but rather by micro-scale surface roughness consisting of microscopic papillae covered in hydrophobic epicuticular wax crystals. Paint manufacturers have commercialized exterior facade coatings (such as Lotusan) that mirror this micro-textured surface, allowing rainwater to continuously wash building exteriors clean without chemical detergents.\n\nParagraph E\nSimilarly, optical engineers have deciphered the structural coloration of Morpho butterfly wings. Unlike paints that rely on chemical pigments that degrade over time under ultraviolet exposure, the iridescent, shimmering blue hue of the Morpho butterfly is created by physical nanostructures that cause constructive optical interference. Light wavelengths other than blue are cancelled out by nano-scale photonic diffraction gratings. This physical principle has unlocked fade-proof paints, high-security anti-counterfeiting banknotes, and ultra-reflective, low-power electronic e-reader displays.\n\nParagraph F\nAs modern civilization confronts resource depletion and climate instability, biomimicry represents a profound paradigm shift: transitioning from an extractive model of 'what can we exploit from nature' to an epistemological model of 'what can we learn from nature's proven designs'.",
