        "lv": "B2",
        "t": "tfng",
        "ctx": "IELTS Academic Reading Passage 3 (Questions 27–40)\nYou should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 27–40, which are based on Reading Passage 3 below.\n\nNEUROPLASTICITY AND BILINGUAL COGNITIVE RESERVE ACROSS THE LIFESPAN\n\nFor much of the twentieth century, conventional neurological dogma held that the adult mammalian brain was a static, hard-wired organ. Once childhood critical developmental windows closed, neuronal pathways were thought to be immutable, with aging characterized by an inexorable, irreversible loss of synaptic density. However, modern functional neuroimaging—particularly functional Magnetic Resonance Imaging (fMRI) and Diffusion Tensor Imaging (DTI)—has demolished this fatalistic paradigm. Contemporary cognitive neuroscience now celebrates 'neuroplasticity': the structural and functional capacity of neural networks to continually reorganize, form novel dendritic arborizations, and reassign cortical functions in response to experiential learning and environmental demands.\n\nAmong the myriad environmental stimuli that shape neuroarchitecture, bilingualism and multilingualism represent two of the most profound and pervasive mental training regimens available to humans. In recent decades, pioneering cognitive psychologists such as Dr. Ellen Bialystok have uncovered compelling evidence that managing two or more linguistic codes simultaneously confers substantial structural and functional neuroprotective advantages, collectively termed 'cognitive reserve'.\n\nIn a bilingual brain, both linguistic systems remain perpetually active in parallel, even during communicative contexts where only one language is being spoken or comprehended. When a bilingual individual intends to utter a sentence in English, the corresponding lexical, syntactic, and phonological representations in their second language (for example, Spanish or Vietnamese) are primed and competing for selection. Consequently, the brain must continuously deploy executive control mechanisms—specifically mediated by the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex, the anterior cingulate cortex, and the basal ganglia—to suppress interference from the non-target language while activating the target language.\n\nThis relentless, lifelong recruitment of executive inhibition networks functions analogously to rigorous resistance weight training for the brain's cognitive musculature. Longitudinal behavioral assays demonstrate that bilingual individuals consistently outperform monolingual cohorts on non-linguistic tasks that assess attentional switching, conflict resolution, and working memory manipulation (such as the Stroop color-word test and the Simon spatial task). Furthermore, neurostructural MRI scans reveal that lifelong bilinguals exhibit significantly greater white matter integrity in the superior longitudinal fasciculus and greater gray matter volume in the anterior cingulate cortex compared to age-matched monolinguals.\n\nPerhaps the most dramatic clinical manifestation of this structural reinforcement is observed in neurodegenerative pathologies. Epidemiological investigations conducted across diverse healthcare systems in Canada, India, and the United Kingdom have consistently documented that lifelong bilingual individuals develop overt clinical symptoms of Alzheimer's disease and vascular dementia an average of 4.5 to 5 years later than their monolingual peers. Crucially, post-mortem neuropathological examinations indicate that bilingual Alzheimer's patients possess equivalent or even greater amounts of beta-amyloid plaques and neurofibrillary tau tangles in brain tissue compared to monolinguals at identical clinical stages. This striking disparity demonstrates that bilingualism does not prevent underlying histological degeneration; rather, the enhanced neuroplastic connectivity and alternative compensatory neural pathways enable bilingual individuals to maintain functional cognitive lucidity far longer despite identical physical pathology.",
        "q": "Reading Passage 3 (Câu 27): Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer in Reading Passage 3?\nWrite TRUE (YES) if the statement agrees with the views of the writer, FALSE (NO) if the statement contradicts the views of the writer, or NOT GIVEN if it is impossible to say what the writer thinks about this.\n\n\"Twentieth-century neurology believed the adult human brain could easily generate new neural pathways.\"",
