        "lv": "B2",
        "t": "tfng",
        "ctx": "IELTS Academic Reading Passage 1 (Questions 1–13)\nYou should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 1–13, which are based on Reading Passage 1 below.\n\nTHE ARCHAEOLOGICAL REDISCOVERY OF THE INDUS VALLEY CIVILIZATION\n\nSpanning modern-day Pakistan, northwest India, and eastern Afghanistan, the Indus Valley (or Harappan) Civilization flourished between 2600 BCE and 1900 BCE, covering an expanse geographically larger than ancient Egypt and Mesopotamia combined. Despite its immense geographic extent and population, estimated at upwards of five million people, the civilization vanished from historical consciousness for millennia until pioneering excavations in the 1920s led by British archaeologist Sir John Marshall at Harappa and Mohenjo-daro unveiled its sophisticated urban ruins.\n\nWhat distinguishes Indus cities from their contemporary Bronze Age counterparts is an astonishing degree of civic standardization and egalitarian urban planning. At Mohenjo-daro and Harappa, streets were laid out in precise orthogonal grid patterns oriented strictly along cardinal directions. Unlike Egypt or Babylon, where immense resources were commandeered to construct colossal royal palaces, monumental pyramids, and militaristic triumph monuments, Indus settlements have yielded virtually no monumental temples or autocratic residences. Instead, civic investment centered squarely on public hygiene, communal sanitation, and standardized civil engineering.\n\nResidential architecture typically comprised multi-story fired-brick houses built around private central courtyards. Most remarkably, nearly every domestic dwelling possessed a dedicated bathing chamber connected to an advanced subterranean municipal drainage network. Covered terracotta effluent drains ran beneath street centerlines, equipped with inspection sumps and limestone silt traps designed for periodic civic maintenance—a sanitation standard unmatched anywhere in the world until the sanitary reforms of nineteenth-century Victorian London.\n\nMetrological uniformity was equally pervasive throughout the civilization. Hundreds of polished chert stone cubical weights discovered across sites thousands of kilometers apart adhere to an identical binary and decimal ratio system. Standardized kiln-fired clay bricks maintained a strict dimensional ratio of 1:2:4 (typically 7 x 14 x 28 centimeters), ensuring optimal structural shear strength in masonry construction.\n\nYet, despite this technical mastery, the Indus script remains undeciphered, preserved on thousands of miniature steatite stamp seals depicting mythical beasts, humped bulls, and enigmatic pictograms. Around 1900 BCE, this magnificent civilization began a precipitous decline. Early twentieth-century historians hypothesized a catastrophic Aryan military invasion; however, modern multidisciplinary paleoclimatic analysis has largely debunked this narrative. Sediment cores from the Arabian Sea and paleochannel mapping reveal that shifting tectonic plates redirected key river systems—most notably the Ghaggar-Hakra river—while an intensifying multi-century monsoon drought undermined the agricultural surplus required to sustain dense urban populations, prompting a gradual dispersal into agrarian agrarian hamlets.",
