        "lv": "B2",
        "t": "mc",
        "ctx": "IELTS Academic Reading Passage 2 (Questions 14–26)\nYou should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 14–26, which are based on Reading Passage 2 below.\n\nDEEP GEOTHERMAL ENERGY AND SUBTERRANEAN HEAT MINING\n\nParagraph A\nAs global energy grids accelerate their transition away from carbon-intensive fossil fuels, renewable alternatives such as wind and solar photovoltaic power have achieved historic commercial deployment. However, these atmospheric renewable sources suffer from inherent intermittency: solar arrays produce no electricity nocturnally, while wind turbine generation fluctuates unpredictably with weather patterns. Consequently, energy economists and grid engineers are directing urgent attention toward deep geothermal energy—a continuous, baseload, zero-carbon power source originating from the vast thermal reservoir of the Earth's molten core and radioactive decay in the continental crust.\n\nParagraph B\nHistorically, conventional geothermal energy has been geographically constrained to volcanically active tectonic plate margins—such as Iceland, New Zealand, the East African Rift, and parts of California—where naturally permeable hydrothermal reservoirs contain boiling water or dry steam relatively close to the surface. However, these conventional 'hydrothermal sweet spots' represent less than two percent of the Earth's total landmass. Over 98 percent of the planet's subterranean heat is locked in dry, impermeable crystalline basement granite rocks buried three to eight kilometers beneath the surface, far from natural water aquifers.\n\nParagraph C\nTo unlock this boundless subterranean reservoir, drilling engineers have pioneered Enhanced Geothermal Systems (EGS). In an EGS project, high-pressure water is injected down a deep borehole to induce micro-shear fractures within hot, dry crystalline rock—a process known as hydraulic stimulation. A secondary production borehole is drilled hundreds of meters away to intersect this artificial fractured heat-exchange network. Cold water circulated down the injection well percolates through the intensely hot subterranean rock matrix, absorbing thermal energy, before ascending through the production well at temperatures exceeding 200°C to drive steam turbine generators on the surface.\n\nParagraph D\nRecent technological breakthroughs borrowed from offshore oil and gas engineering have dramatically expanded EGS commercial viability. In particular, directional horizontal drilling and advanced polycrystalline diamond compact (PDC) drill bits allow operators to steer boreholes horizontally through deep granite for several kilometers, vastly multiplying the subterranean surface area exposed to circulating fluids. Furthermore, innovative 'closed-loop' advanced geothermal designs circulate supercritical fluids (such as supercritical carbon dioxide) through sealed subterranean radiator loops, eliminating the risk of subsurface fluid loss or induced seismic micro-tremors.\n\nParagraph E\nDespite these engineering triumphs, significant economic and geological hurdles remain. Deep drilling through ultra-abrasive basement granite at temperatures exceeding 250°C accelerates drill bit wear, pushing capital drilling expenditures to tens of millions of dollars per well. In addition, poorly monitored hydraulic stimulation in fault-prone regions has historically triggered noticeable induced seismicity—such as the 2006 Basel geothermal project in Switzerland, which was terminated after an induced 3.4 magnitude earthquake alarmed local residents.\n\nParagraph F\nNevertheless, the thermodynamic potential of deep geothermal remains staggering. The United States Department of Energy estimates that harvesting just two percent of the thermal energy stored in crystalline basement rock beneath the continental United States between depths of 3 and 10 kilometers would yield more than 2,000 times the nation's total annual energy consumption, offering an inexhaustible, dispatchable baseload energy anchor for a fully decarbonized global economy.",
