        "t": "tfng",
        "ctx": "IELTS Academic Reading Passage 3 (Questions 27–40)\nYou should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 27–40, which are based on Reading Passage 3 below.\n\nARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE, EPISTEMOLOGY, AND HUMAN INTUITION\n\nThe meteoric ascension of deep artificial neural networks and large foundational models has precipitated a profound philosophical dilemma within epistemology—the philosophical study of knowledge and justified belief. For centuries, the Western scientific method, formulated by Enlightenment thinkers such as Francis Bacon and René Descartes, was founded upon deductive rationalism and mechanistic explainability. A scientific theory was deemed valid only if human intellect could trace each causal link from foundational first principles to observable empirical phenomena.\n\nContemporary deep learning architectures, however, have fundamentally decoupled prediction from comprehension. Modern transformer models and deep reinforcement learning systems operate with hundreds of billions of numerical parameters distributed across multilayered non-linear matrix transformations. When an advanced neural network diagnoses complex medical radiographs with diagnostic accuracy exceeding board-certified radiologists, or folds biological proteins with atomic precision, it does not do so by articulating intelligible biochemical rules. Instead, it computes multidimensional statistical correlations across latent mathematical manifolds that entirely elude human cognitive representation—a phenomenon epistemologists term the 'black box problem'.\n\nThis epistemic opacity has divided the scientific community into two stark ideological camps. The pragmatic instrumentalists contend that in domains characterized by high-dimensional complexity—such as climate modeling, genomic medicine, and quantum chemistry—human cognitive capacity is inherently inadequate. For instrumentalists, predictive utility is the supreme epistemological criterion: if an algorithmic model consistently produces accurate, reproducible, and verifiable empirical predictions, demanding that it be reducible to simplistic human-interpretable heuristics is an anachronistic anthropocentric conceit.\n\nConversely, rationalist purists argue that conflating prediction with true scientific understanding poses perilous intellectual hazards. Without mechanistic transparency, artificial systems are prone to 'hallucinations', adversarial vulnerabilities, and deceptive statistical shortcuts—such as classifying an image of a dog as a wolf simply because the training photographs invariably featured snow in the background. More fundamentally, rationalists contend that science is not merely a tool for forecasting outcomes, but an ontological quest to understand the underlying fabric of physical reality. When an algorithmic system provides an answer without an explanatory derivation, it produces 'oracular knowledge'—analogous to consulting the Pythian Oracle of ancient Delphi, whose prophecies were taken on faith without causal illumination.\n\nBridging this epistemic chasm represents the central mandate of Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI). Computer scientists and cognitive philosophers are developing post-hoc interpretability algorithms, attention-heatmapping tools, and mechanistic interpretability probes designed to reverse-engineer the internal representations of deep networks. The ultimate objective is not to constrain artificial intelligence to the limits of biological human intuition, but to establish a reciprocal cognitive partnership: where artificial systems illuminate emergent patterns within the cosmos, while human scientific reasoning interrogates and conceptualizes the underlying principles.",
        "q": "Reading Passage 3 (Câu 27): Do the following statements agree with the views of the writer in Reading Passage 3?\nWrite TRUE (YES) if the statement agrees, FALSE (NO) if it contradicts, or NOT GIVEN if it is impossible to say.\n\n\"Enlightenment thinkers believed scientific validity required human intellect to understand causal mechanisms.\"",
        "a": "T",
